WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:01.291
- Good morning.

00:00:01.291 --> 00:00:02.917
- [Audience] Good morning.

00:00:02.917 --> 00:00:05.375
- So, I'll give a
little spiel, but honestly,

00:00:05.375 --> 00:00:07.083
I think given the
size of the audience,

00:00:07.083 --> 00:00:09.917
and given the vibe here,
I think we should go into Q&amp;amp;A.

00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:12.166
I think that's
where the more interesting

00:00:12.166 --> 00:00:13.500
value comes out of.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.792
But, to create a
little framework.

00:00:15.792 --> 00:00:17.000
I am an immigrant.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.625
I was born in Belarus
in the former Soviet Union.

00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:22.291
I came here when I was three.

00:00:22.291 --> 00:00:25.250
I lived in Queens, in
Rego Park for two years,

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.291
in a studio
apartment with five, six,

00:00:27.291 --> 00:00:29.458
seven, eight family members.

00:00:29.458 --> 00:00:32.417
So, I can relate to
that grind and that hustle.

00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:34.125
I literally didn't
even know my dad

00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:36.083
for the first
14 years of my life,

00:00:36.083 --> 00:00:37.834
because he would
leave before I would wake up

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:39.625
and get home
after I fell asleep.

00:00:39.625 --> 00:00:41.667
First as a stock boy
in a liquor store

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:42.959
making two bucks an hour,

00:00:42.959 --> 00:00:46.125
and then eventually a manager.

00:00:46.125 --> 00:00:48.041
And immigrants, and I'm
sure some of you know this,

00:00:48.041 --> 00:00:51.375
immigrants have
really figured out the secret

00:00:51.375 --> 00:00:53.625
of American Business.

00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:55.959
Which is don't spend any money

00:00:55.959 --> 00:00:58.792
on dumb shit for 10 years,

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:01.208
save it all, and
then buy something, right?

00:01:01.208 --> 00:01:03.959
And so, that's what my dad did.

00:01:03.959 --> 00:01:05.625
And he bought a
small liquor store

00:01:05.625 --> 00:01:07.667
in Springfield, New Jersey.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.959
I grew up in Edison, New Jersey.

00:01:09.959 --> 00:01:14.208
You know, lemonade stands,
shoveling snow, baseball cards.

00:01:15.291 --> 00:01:19.250
You know, it's so crazy
for me that I get to live

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.083
through the era where
an entrepreneur is cool.

00:01:23.959 --> 00:01:28.708
The fact that I take 10 selfies
a day blows my fuckin' mind.

00:01:28.708 --> 00:01:33.208
The fact that hip hop
artists that I love DM me.

00:01:33.208 --> 00:01:34.625
It's just so crazy.

00:01:34.625 --> 00:01:35.667
And it's happened, right?

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:37.208
It's happened for sports.

00:01:37.208 --> 00:01:38.667
I think a lot of people forget.

00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:42.583
In the 1950s, baseball
players and football players

00:01:42.583 --> 00:01:46.125
had jobs during the
summer at hardware stores.

00:01:46.125 --> 00:01:47.750
Everything gets it's day,

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.583
and I think there's
nothing more Americana

00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:51.500
than the entrepreneur,
than the business person

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.375
and so, I'm just fortunate.

00:01:53.375 --> 00:01:56.166
You know, when I was
your age, I was a loser

00:01:56.166 --> 00:01:58.250
by a lot of people's POV,

00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.959
because I was
a terrible student.

00:01:59.959 --> 00:02:04.125
And the narrative 25 years
ago was that entrepreneurship

00:02:04.125 --> 00:02:06.333
was not a feasible way out.

00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:10.959
That the only way out was
Yale and Harvard and Cornell.

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:13.959
School was the only benchmark
that created opportunity

00:02:13.959 --> 00:02:15.125
and so for me,

00:02:17.125 --> 00:02:19.375
I'm excited about
this entrepreneurial thing,

00:02:19.375 --> 00:02:21.834
but at the same token,
to be very frank,

00:02:21.834 --> 00:02:23.458
one of the things that
I want to try to dissect

00:02:23.458 --> 00:02:25.041
in our Q&amp;amp;A now is,

00:02:25.041 --> 00:02:26.625
I also think it's
gone a little bit too far

00:02:26.625 --> 00:02:28.083
in the other direction.

00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:29.875
I think everybody
thinks they're going

00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:31.667
to be a winning entrepreneur.

00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:32.500
You know, it's funny.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.875
Everybody says
they're an entrepreneur.

00:02:33.875 --> 00:02:35.875
That's like me saying
I'm a basketball player.

00:02:35.875 --> 00:02:37.333
That's cool.

00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:39.834
It's fun that I get to run at
the YMCA a couple of times.

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:41.250
But, I don't get paid.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.458
Right, I'm not an NBA All-Star.

00:02:43.458 --> 00:02:44.875
And what I think is
happening right now is

00:02:44.875 --> 00:02:47.041
people are putting
the word entrepreneur

00:02:47.041 --> 00:02:50.875
in their Instagram profile
and they think it's a wrap.

00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:52.792
They think they're going to
make it and this and that.

00:02:52.792 --> 00:02:54.417
And it's extremely hard.

00:02:54.417 --> 00:02:57.000
It's extremely lonely.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.917
Listen, if you want this,
you need to understand

00:03:00.917 --> 00:03:01.792
that there's a lot of stuff

00:03:01.792 --> 00:03:03.250
that people are
not talking about.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.166
Nobody in my tech
startup world is talking

00:03:05.166 --> 00:03:08.458
about the suicides that
are happening when kids fail.

00:03:08.458 --> 00:03:10.333
Maple, the food service

00:03:10.333 --> 00:03:12.542
that was supposed to be
so big, folded yesterday.

00:03:12.542 --> 00:03:14.291
This is happening
every day. Right?

00:03:14.291 --> 00:03:18.333
And everybody
thinks it's so easy,

00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:20.208
and the reason
they think it's so easy

00:03:20.208 --> 00:03:23.291
is I grew up,
when I was your age,

00:03:23.291 --> 00:03:24.792
you couldn't roll
up on people and say

00:03:24.792 --> 00:03:27.750
here's my idea and they'd
give you a million dollars

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.625
on a $4 million valuation.

00:03:30.625 --> 00:03:33.291
That was insane,
that's not how it was.

00:03:33.291 --> 00:03:35.333
And I think the
other thing I'm fearful of,

00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:36.417
especially when
I look around the room,

00:03:36.417 --> 00:03:40.291
there's a couple of us,
but for the majority of you,

00:03:40.291 --> 00:03:41.875
we've had a good
economy now for the last

00:03:41.875 --> 00:03:43.667
seven or eight years.

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.417
And for a lot of you,
seven or eight years ago,

00:03:45.417 --> 00:03:46.708
you were a real youngster.

00:03:46.708 --> 00:03:50.542
So you haven't lived
through when the world melts.

00:03:50.542 --> 00:03:52.750
I lived through the
collapse of .com, 2000.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.333
And then right behind it 9/11.

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:56.208
And then 2007, and eight.

00:03:56.208 --> 00:03:58.375
And I've navigated my
businesses through that.

00:03:58.375 --> 00:03:59.583
That's hard.

00:03:59.583 --> 00:04:01.625
Everybody's a
peacetime general,

00:04:01.625 --> 00:04:03.208
but who's a wartime general?

00:04:03.208 --> 00:04:07.166
Everybody's really great,
everybody's a hero

00:04:07.166 --> 00:04:09.834
when you can print
a logo on a t-shirt

00:04:09.834 --> 00:04:12.667
and say I've
got a fashion brand. Right?

00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:14.959
But what about actually
building something sustainable

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:16.291
that you can eat on
and things of that nature?

00:04:16.291 --> 00:04:19.500
So, for me, I'm not
trying to discourage,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.666
I'm just trying to
paint a very real picture.

00:04:21.666 --> 00:04:23.291
This is a long game.

00:04:23.291 --> 00:04:27.959
Like, from 22 to 30,
I did nothing.

00:04:27.959 --> 00:04:31.208
I worked 15 hours a day,
Monday through Saturday.

00:04:31.208 --> 00:04:32.333
You know, it's funny.

00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:35.250
I was talking at a
talk the other day,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.250
it was a Friday.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.125
I go, "Tomorrow,
more of you are going to

00:04:38.125 --> 00:04:40.917
"have more Saturdays
off in your 20s than I did

00:04:40.917 --> 00:04:43.333
"in my entire 20s, tomorrow."

00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:46.583
Because Saturday is
the biggest day of retail

00:04:46.583 --> 00:04:48.750
and that's when we
sold the most wine,

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.291
so I was just there
every single Saturday

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:54.583
of every 20 to 30
years old, 10 years.

00:04:54.583 --> 00:04:56.291
Every fuckin' Saturday.

00:04:56.291 --> 00:04:58.250
And so when people,

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.083
all the cliche
shit that we all talk,

00:05:00.083 --> 00:05:02.166
"I'm grindin'," "I'm hustlin',"

00:05:02.166 --> 00:05:03.333
you know, like "I'm doin' this."

00:05:03.333 --> 00:05:06.125
I laugh because I
look at people's Insta

00:05:06.125 --> 00:05:08.291
and they're
fucking at Coachella.

00:05:08.291 --> 00:05:11.333
(audience laughter)

00:05:11.333 --> 00:05:13.583
So I think there's a mix.

00:05:13.583 --> 00:05:16.000
There's a mix
because entrepreneurship

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.000
has also gotten cool.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.625
I said something at a talk
that really resonates with me.

00:05:20.625 --> 00:05:22.625
And she's like,
"Oh fuck, club promoters

00:05:22.625 --> 00:05:24.917
"have taken over
entrepreneurship." Right?

00:05:24.917 --> 00:05:27.250
We're putting it
on this pedestal.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:31.041
And now, with the way that
Instagram dominates our world,

00:05:31.041 --> 00:05:33.792
and the visuals of
watches and private jets,

00:05:33.792 --> 00:05:35.458
and girls and guys,

00:05:35.458 --> 00:05:37.208
and boats and champagne,

00:05:37.208 --> 00:05:38.458
I'm just like, "Fuck."

00:05:38.458 --> 00:05:40.417
99% of people are gonna lose.

00:05:40.417 --> 00:05:41.750
And what I'm scared of,

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:46.083
is if people actually knew
what to do, which is eat shit,

00:05:46.083 --> 00:05:48.959
like work real hard,
be real patient.

00:05:48.959 --> 00:05:51.458
You know what I would
wish on you more than anything?

00:05:51.458 --> 00:05:52.792
More than anything.

00:05:52.792 --> 00:05:55.208
And I'm looking around the room.

00:05:55.208 --> 00:05:57.583
Our wonderful cops back
there, a couple people here.

00:05:57.583 --> 00:05:59.083
There's seven or
eight of us that know this,

00:05:59.083 --> 00:05:59.917
the rest of you don't,

00:05:59.917 --> 00:06:03.125
which is if you knew that at 40,

00:06:03.125 --> 00:06:06.417
you felt the same way
inside as you do at 20,

00:06:06.417 --> 00:06:08.625
it would fuck with your head.

00:06:08.625 --> 00:06:10.542
If you actually knew how on fire

00:06:10.542 --> 00:06:12.625
like, I think we're friends.

00:06:12.625 --> 00:06:14.000
Like, I think
we're the same age.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:14.834
It's crazy.

00:06:14.834 --> 00:06:16.792
When I was 22, my
cousin was in the business.

00:06:16.792 --> 00:06:18.875
He was 30, he was
eight years older than me.

00:06:18.875 --> 00:06:21.542
I thought he was old as fuck.

00:06:21.542 --> 00:06:22.750
(audience laughter)

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:25.041
I remember, that's
not super long ago.

00:06:25.041 --> 00:06:26.166
I was 22, he was 30.

00:06:26.166 --> 00:06:27.667
He was old as shit.

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:29.542
So when I sit here and I'm 41,

00:06:29.542 --> 00:06:32.125
I'm like, "Fuck, these kids
think I'm real fucking old."

00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:33.166
(audience laughter)

00:06:33.166 --> 00:06:36.875
And I feel fucking
as young as you.

00:06:36.875 --> 00:06:40.250
And if you knew that,
if you knew that,

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.208
you would get way more patient.

00:06:43.208 --> 00:06:45.250
If you knew that, you
would get way more patient.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.417
I promise you,
the number one thing

00:06:47.417 --> 00:06:49.375
that I'm trying
to leave here with

00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:52.959
is to get a couple of you
to get real fucking patient

00:06:52.959 --> 00:06:55.667
'cause that is the
singular advantage.

00:06:55.667 --> 00:06:58.500
If you actually
don't give a fuck,

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.959
the way I didn't, of what
people think about you,

00:07:01.959 --> 00:07:05.083
all through your 20s,
like you just don't care,

00:07:05.083 --> 00:07:06.750
like you literally don't,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.959
and you just build
something for yourself,

00:07:08.959 --> 00:07:10.750
you have a much better chance.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:12.500
So many people here will fail

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.417
in their
entrepreneurial journeys

00:07:15.417 --> 00:07:18.166
because they're worried
about what people think.

00:07:18.166 --> 00:07:19.959
That's just what it is, my man.

00:07:19.959 --> 00:07:21.375
That's just what it is.

00:07:21.375 --> 00:07:22.917
People are worried
about what other people think,

00:07:22.917 --> 00:07:23.959
how many followers they have,

00:07:23.959 --> 00:07:24.917
how good the business is doing,

00:07:24.917 --> 00:07:26.125
what they're doing,
where they're going,

00:07:26.125 --> 00:07:27.250
what they're wearing.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:28.917
It doesn't fucking matter.

00:07:28.917 --> 00:07:30.250
It especially doesn't matter

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.250
if you pour all that
energy into building something

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:36.417
and then at 33 you're
winning, and they're resetting.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:41.417
(audience cheers and applause)

00:07:42.417 --> 00:07:46.250
And if you really knew
how young you would be at 33,

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.125
it would really
change everything.

00:07:48.125 --> 00:07:49.750
Like, that's the thing.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:51.708
I'm trying to really
put the words together

00:07:51.708 --> 00:07:53.875
'cause I'm going
back to that time.

00:07:53.875 --> 00:07:55.083
It's just hard. Right?

00:07:55.083 --> 00:07:58.041
It's hard when
you're that young.

00:07:58.041 --> 00:08:01.250
It's hard to realize
you'll feel that young.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.834
I just don't know how to say it.

00:08:02.834 --> 00:08:04.250
I really don't
know how to say it.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:07.500
I don't, but I want to
say it with conviction,

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.166
so that a couple
of you believe me.

00:08:09.166 --> 00:08:12.583
But you have to build an actual
business that makes money.

00:08:12.583 --> 00:08:14.250
If you're going
into the tech business,

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:16.875
if you want to
build an app, it's hard.

00:08:16.875 --> 00:08:19.542
For every single
Snapchat and Instagram,

00:08:19.542 --> 00:08:23.375
there's eight million
Insta-shits. You know?

00:08:23.375 --> 00:08:24.750
Everybody fails.

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:27.917
And we get seduced by the two
or three people that don't.

00:08:27.917 --> 00:08:28.959
It's really funny.

00:08:28.959 --> 00:08:31.917
I went to Mount Ida College.

00:08:31.917 --> 00:08:35.833
It was 94%
African-American, Latino,

00:08:35.833 --> 00:08:37.166
real minority college.

00:08:37.166 --> 00:08:40.792
And every single
person wanted to be a rapper.

00:08:40.792 --> 00:08:42.000
It was 1994.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.792
Everybody was gonna
and I remember thinking,

00:08:43.792 --> 00:08:46.792
"Wow, this is
some delusional shit."

00:08:47.875 --> 00:08:49.834
How are you gonna be a
rapper if you're not writing?

00:08:49.834 --> 00:08:51.708
How are you gonna be a rapper
if you're not in the studio?

00:08:51.708 --> 00:08:53.792
How are you gonna be a
rapper if you're not hustling?

00:08:53.792 --> 00:08:56.625
And it's funny,
like, the white boy version

00:08:56.625 --> 00:08:59.166
